      </header>

      <div class="prose">
        <h2>Summary</h2>

        {/*
          Deliberately small and floated rather than a hero image. The page is
          a research story; a full-width photograph of people would make it
          read as a profile instead. It sits in the summary because that is
          where a reader has just learned what the project is, and where
          saying who it is done with adds context rather than interrupting.
        */}
        <figure class="photo-inset">
          <img
            src="/work/qec/advisors.jpg"
            alt="Three people standing together in a laboratory office corridor, all wearing Fermilab lanyards."
            width="1200"
            height="897"
            loading="lazy"
            decoding="async"
          />
          <figcaption>
            With my research advisors at Fermilab: Dr. Gabriel Perdue, Senior
            Scientist, and Dr. Giuseppe Di Guglielmo, Principal Engineer.
          </figcaption>
        </figure>

        <p>
          A quantum computer cannot look at its own state to see whether it has
          been damaged — looking destroys it. Instead it measures a set of
          carefully chosen parities that reveal that something went wrong while
          revealing nothing about the encoded information. The catch is that
          those measurements are also nearly silent about the error itself:
          many different physical errors produce exactly the same measurement
          outcome, and a decoder has to choose between them, correctly, in
          about a microsecond, forever.
        </p>
        <p>
          The standard answer is minimum-weight perfect matching, which assumes
          the simplest explanation is the right one. It is fast, it is
          principled, and it is provably not optimal. Neural decoders can
          recover some of what matching throws away. This project is an
          experimental study of when they do: how the advantage responds to
          model architecture, to how much data the model has seen, to the size
          of the code being protected, and to the severe constraints of
          hardware that has to keep pace with the machine it is correcting.
        </p>

        <h2>The research question</h2>
        <p>
          Matching picks the single most likely error <em>chain</em>. What
          determines whether the logical qubit survives is the most likely
          error <em>class</em> — the whole family of chains that would need the
          same correction. Those two are not the same, and the gap between them
          is where an optimal decoder lives.
        </p>
        <p>
          So the question is not simply whether a network can beat matching.
          It is: <strong>how much of the structure matching misses do neural
          decoders actually capture, and what happens to that as the
          architecture, the training scale, the code configuration and the
          hardware budget change?</strong>
        </p>
        <p>
          Those four turn out to pull against each other. A model large enough
          to learn the structure may be too large to run inside the correction
          cycle; an advantage measured on a small code at one noise strength
          may not survive at a larger one. The useful result is not a single
          number but the shape of those trade-offs.
        </p>

