        <h3>Try it</h3>
        <p>
          Put a few errors in a line and watch the interior checks fall silent.
          A chain of length one and a chain of length five can leave the same
          two checks lit — that is the ambiguity the decoder has to resolve, and
          it is not a limitation of the measurement apparatus but of what a
          parity check is.
        </p>

        <SurfaceCode />

        <h2>Method</h2>
        <h3>Data</h3>
        <p>
          Syndromes were generated with circuit-level noise rather than a
          simplified independent-error model — the measurement apparatus is
          itself faulty, so the syndrome arrives as a noisy time series over
          repeated rounds and not as one clean snapshot. Training used tens of
          millions of shots; the model is only converged at that scale, and
          conclusions drawn from smaller runs were, repeatedly, artifacts.
        </p>

        <h3>Model</h3>
        <p>
          This project builds on a recurrent–convolutional (RCNN) decoder
          developed by Ulaşcan. The architecture combines a recurrent stage
          that processes syndrome rounds over time with a convolutional stage
          that exploits the spatial structure of the surface-code lattice. It
          predicts one bit — whether a logical flip has occurred — because that
          is the only question with a well-defined answer.
        </p>
        <p>
          My work focuses on experimentally evaluating that decoder: how its
          performance scales with training data and code configuration, how it
          compares with simpler MLP and GRU architectures, and how much it can
          be compressed for eventual FPGA deployment.
        </p>

